  We propose an **AI-powered chat and reporting platform** to help a bank understand technology changes across its credit-card products, applications, services, and third-party vendors. It will help teams review past changes, track delivery progress, and assess what a planned upgrade could affect.

Users can ask questions in plain English, such as: “What happened during similar upgrades?”, “Which vendor has had more recurring issues?”, or “What should we check before upgrading this authorization service?” The platform will find relevant records, calculate the required measures, and present an explanation alongside charts, tables, and supporting evidence.

**The chat and dashboard will work together.** A user could ask to compare two vendors, narrow the results to one service, or show changes from the last six months. The charts and explanation would update with the conversation. Users could then open the underlying Jira tickets to investigate particular findings.

Initially, the platform will use **Jira stories, bugs, comments, ticket links, and status history**. This will support analysis of delivery progress, delays, recurring problems, and similar historical work. Jira alone may not show whether a change caused a production failure or affected customers. Where that evidence is missing, the platform will clearly say that the impact is uncertain.

Over time, we can connect incident records, release data, application dependencies, vendor performance, service monitoring, and customer complaints. These sources will help teams identify downstream systems that may be affected and assess whether similar changes previously had positive, neutral, or negative outcomes. Historical similarities will support an assessment, rather than prove what will happen next.

The solution will use **Amazon Bedrock for AI analysis and explanations**, with controlled data tools performing calculations. Coordinated AI agents will divide the work: understand the question, retrieve comparable changes, analyze the data, assess potential risks, and prepare the relevant visual view. Shared business definitions will keep measures consistent as new data sources are added.

Every important finding will include its supporting sources, assumptions, and missing information. Users will only see data they are authorized to access, and the platform will record its queries and results for audit and review.

